; What a bare `dune build` builds, and what an install carries.
;
; Left implicit, the root's default alias is every installable target, which
; reaches the libraries, the executable and the test suites but not assets/.
; The pictures still have to reach _build, because that is where Asset finds
; them: one directory above the executable, which under dune is _build/default.
; So the alias is spelled out, and `dune build` is the whole repository rather
; than only the parts of it opam would carry.
;
; The pictures belong to the camlcast-demo package and not to camlcast: the
; engine reads no file and has nothing to put in a prefix, and the program that
; does read files has to carry them. Under the prefix that is
; share/camlcast-demo/assets, one directory above bin/ and across — the same
; shape as a macOS bundle, which is why Asset finds it without being told. It is
; the source pictures that go, not a copy of the build: they are what
; tools/bundle-*.sh carry into a .app or an AppImage as well.

(alias
 (name default)
 (deps
  (alias_rec all)
  (source_tree assets)))

(install
 (package camlcast-demo)
 (section share)
 (files
  (glob_files
   (assets/*.png with_prefix assets))))

; Turn the unused-code warnings back on, because dune stopped doing it for us.
;
; Under (lang dune 3.0) the dev profile compiled with
; `-w @1..3@5..28@30..39...`, which enabled warning 32 and made it fatal. At the
; 3.21 this project has declared since its first commit, dune passes no -w at
; all: the command line is `-short-paths -keep-locs -warn-error +a`, so the
; compiler's own defaults apply — and those switch 32..42 off. -warn-error +a
; only escalates warnings that are enabled, so the whole unused-* family was
; silently unreachable rather than merely non-fatal.
;
; It cost something. Bitmap.alpha survived the sealing commit that un-exported
; it, dead and unreferenced, and nothing anywhere said so. This is the check
; that would have caught it the moment it stopped being called.
;
; 32..39 is the unused-* family: value, open, type, for-loop index, ancestor,
; constructor, extension constructor, rec flag. 9 catches a record pattern that
; does not name every field, which is what turns adding a field into a compile
; error at each place that should have handled it rather than a silent hole; 27
; catches a bound name nothing reads. Both were one site each when they went on.
;
; What is left off is left off on evidence. Building this tree with -w +a raises
; 467 of warning 42, 257 of 40 and 157 of 44 — the constructor/label
; disambiguation family, which fires on correct idiomatic code and which dune's
; own default excluded for the same reason — and 55 of warning 4, which asks for
; a style of pattern matching this codebase does not write. 70 is a special
; case; see core/dune and loom/dune, which do switch it on.
;
; -strict-sequence and -strict-formats are here rather than among the warnings
; because they are typing flags: the first makes the left of a `;` have to be
; unit, the second rejects the ambiguous old format-string syntax. Both were in
; dune's dev profile at (lang dune 3.0) and vanished at 3.21 along with the -w,
; and both were already clean here — this only stops that from rotting.
;
; -principal is the one deliberately declined. It is a real check rather than a
; stylistic one, but loom/hook.ml does not typecheck under it, and buying it
; would mean rewriting the part of the reconciler whose inference is subtlest.
;
; :standard keeps -warn-error +a, so these are errors rather than notices; (dev)
; keeps that from reaching --profile release, and so from reaching the `-p`
; builds opam runs, where a warning must never fail someone else's install.

(env
 (dev
  (flags
   (:standard -w +32..39+9+27 -strict-sequence -strict-formats))))
